avif.demuxer/--background--

 DESCRIPTION
   A Reggae demultiplexer for AVIF (AV1 Image File Format) files. It parses
   the ISO Base Media File Format box structure of a file, takes the primary
   image item apart and hands the AV1 bitstream of that item on as
   MMF_VIDEO_AVIF, together with the geometry, the bit depth, the alpha flag
   and whatever metadata it found.

   Input port 0 carries MMF_STREAM and is a source stream in front of it,
   normally file.stream. Output port 1 carries MMF_VIDEO_AVIF, the AV1 OBU
   stream of the image, which avif.decoder takes on its input port 0 and
   decodes to MMFC_VIDEO_ARGB32. The identifier lives in avif.demuxer.h.

   Everything is parsed on MMM_Setup on port 0. The parse walks the top level
   boxes looking for ftyp and meta, buffers the meta box, reads pitm, iinf,
   iloc, iref and iprp out of it, resolves the ispe, pixi, av1C, colr and
   auxC properties of the primary item, finds the alpha item and the XMP
   item, and finally assembles the bitstream by seeking to each extent of the
   item in the input and pulling it. The bitstream is then kept in memory, so
   the file is not touched again: MMM_Pull on port 1 only copies out of that
   buffer.

   The geometry is therefore only known after port 0 has been set up. Before
   that, MMA_Video_Width, MMA_Video_Height and MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel are 0
   and MMA_Video_UseAlpha is FALSE. An application which wants to size its
   buffers beforehand has to set port 0 up before it can ask for the width.

   Setting port 0 up again parses the file from scratch: the previous result
   is dropped, so the pull position on port 1 starts at the beginning of the
   new bitstream and a struct AvifInfo handed out earlier is freed. Asking for
   a geometry attribute is not a substitute for the setup, it only reads what
   the parse has left behind.

   The class needs a source which knows how long it is. The length of the
   input (MMA_StreamLength) bounds every box and every extent that is read,
   so a source which reports 0, or a stream shorter than 16 bytes, is refused
   with MMERR_END_OF_DATA. A file with no ftyp box, no meta box, a corrupt
   meta box, no primary item reference, a primary item which is not an "av01"
   item or has no ispe property, or an item location this class cannot
   resolve, is refused with MMERR_WRONG_DATA. A meta box above 16 MB, a
   bitstream above 64 MB or a failed allocation is MMERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, and a
   source which cannot seek or comes up short is MMERR_IO_ERROR.

   The bitstream on port 1 is the item data as it is stored: the extents
   concatenated in the order the location box lists them. The av1C box is a
   codec configuration record and not an OBU, so it is never prepended to it;
   ai_Av1c in MMA_ExtraData carries it for an application which wants to see
   it. A conforming file begins the item with an OBU sequence header, and a
   stream which does not is logged as a warning but still handed out.

   An image with alpha is coded as a second AV1 item, and that item is not
   emitted on port 1 either: it reaches avif.decoder through MMA_ExtraData,
   which is the only path between the two classes. This class only ever
   reports one frame, so an animated file ('avis') gives the item its primary
   reference names and nothing else. Derived items ('Grid') are not decoded:
   a primary item which is not an AV1 item is refused.

   The class implements no seeking. Nothing is maintained for
   MMA_StreamPosFrames or MMA_StreamPosTime, and MMM_Seek on port 1 is left to
   multimedia.class.

   The recognition code of this class accepts a file whose first box is ftyp
   and whose major brand, or one of whose compatible brands, is "avif",
   "avis", "avio" or "mif1". The compatible brands are checked because
   encoders may use a generic major brand and only list AVIF among the brands
   the file is compatible with. It reports 9500, plus 500 when the stream is
   at least as long as the ftyp box.

   Parsing is bounded: at most 64 items, 64 properties, 32 properties per
   item and 16 extents per item are looked at, which is far above what real
   files use and keeps a corrupt file from asking for an arbitrary
   allocation. The meta box is only buffered up to 16 MB and an assembled
   bitstream only up to 64 MB.

 NEW ATTRIBUTES
   Attributes applicability:
     I - may be set at creation time.
     S - may be set on an existing object.
     G - may be get from an object.
     P - may be set for an object's port.
     Q - may be queried from an object's port.

   AVIF_META_DATA         (V1)    [..G.Q], struct AvifMetaData*
   MMA_DataFormat         (V1)    [..G.Q], STRPTR
   MMA_ExtraData          (V1)    [..G.Q], struct AvifInfo*
   MMA_MediaType          (V1)    [..G.Q], ULONG
   MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel (V1)    [..G.Q], ULONG
   MMA_Video_FrameCount   (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG
   MMA_Video_Height       (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG
   MMA_Video_UseAlpha     (V1)    [..G.Q], BOOL
   MMA_Video_Width        (V1)    [..G.Q], LONG

   All of them answer the parse which MMM_Setup on port 0 has done. None of
   them may be set.

 NEW METHODS
   MMM_Pull(port, buffer, length)  (V1)

 HISTORY
   1.1 (15.09.2026)
   - Initial release.

avif.demuxer/AVIF_META_DATA

 NAME
   AVIF_META_DATA (V1) [..G.Q], struct AvifMetaData*

 FUNCTION
   Returns a pointer to the metadata strings this class has extracted from the
   file. amd_Count of the amd_Entries are valid; amd_Entries[i].ame_Tag is
   AVIF_META_TITLE, AVIF_META_COPYRIGHT or AVIF_META_DESCRIPTION, and
   amd_Entries[i].ame_String is a NUL terminated string owned by this object.

   The strings come from the XMP packet of the file, which is looked for as a
   "mime" item with the content type application/rdf+xml. amd_Count is 0 when
   the file carries no such item, or when it carries one with none of the
   three properties in it.

 NOTES
   This is a private attribute of this class, defined in avif.demuxer.h and
   not published in the attribute list of the library, so an application has
   to ask for the tag by name.

   Only dc:title, dc:rights and dc:description are looked for, in both the
   forms encoders write them in: as an attribute of rdf:Description
   (dc:title="...") and as an element holding an rdf:Alt or rdf:Bag list
   (<dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">...</rdf:li>...
   </rdf:Alt></dc:title>). A string is stored as it stands, without being
   unescaped or translated, and at most 8 entries of 256 bytes each are kept.

   The struct is owned by this object and is freed when the object is
   disposed or when port 0 is set up again. Copy what is needed out of it
   rather than keeping the pointer.

   MMA_MetaData is not used for this. Its storage layout is not visible from
   this repository, so this class does not guess at it.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_ExtraData

avif.demuxer/MMA_DataFormat

 NAME
   MMA_DataFormat (V1) [..G.Q], STRPTR

 FUNCTION
   Returns "AVIF" as a string constant. It names the format of the data on
   the ports rather than anything read from the file, and does not change.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_MediaType

avif.demuxer/MMA_ExtraData

 NAME
   MMA_ExtraData (V1) [..G.Q], struct AvifInfo*

 FUNCTION
   Returns a pointer to the struct AvifInfo which describes the image and owns
   the bitstreams. This is what avif.decoder reads on its setup: the struct
   carries the geometry and the colour signalling the decoder needs, and the
   two AV1 streams it has to decode.

   ai_Width, ai_Height and ai_BitsPerPixel are the geometry and the luma bit
   depth of the image. ai_UseAlpha is TRUE when the file codes the alpha
   channel as a separate item, ai_AlphaPremul when the colour planes were
   premultiplied, ai_Monochrome for a single plane image, and ai_ChromaSubX
   and ai_ChromaSubY the chroma subsampling. ai_ES and ai_ESLength are the
   colour plane OBU stream, ai_AlphaES and ai_AlphaESLength the alpha plane
   one, which are NULL and 0 when the image has no separate alpha. ai_HaveNclx
   says whether the colour fields after it mean anything: ai_ColourPrimaries,
   ai_TransferCharacteristics and ai_MatrixCoefficients are ITU-T H.273 code
   points and ai_FullRangeFlag is the range flag, all as the colr box of the
   file has them. ai_Av1c and ai_Av1cLength are the
   AV1CodecConfigurationRecord of the image.

 NOTES
   The struct belongs to this object. It is allocated on setup, freed on
   dispose, and replaced by the next setup of port 0; ai_ES and ai_AlphaES
   point into memory this object owns and must not be freed or written to.

   The pull position is kept in the object and not in the struct, so ai_ES
   always covers the whole stream and asking for this attribute twice returns
   the same stream both times.

   The value is a pointer, so it has to be fetched through a storage pointer
   (MediaGetPort() or MediaGetPortFwd() on the input port, or a GetAttr()
   with a pointer as its storage): it cannot be carried in the 32 bit return
   value of a MorphOS method call.

   The definition is in avif.demuxer.h.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/AVIF_META_DATA, avif.decoder/MMM_Pull

avif.demuxer/MMA_MediaType

 NAME
   MMA_MediaType (V1) [..G.Q], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Returns MMT_PICTURE. AVIF still images are pictures and this class always
   reports one of them.

avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel

 NAME
   MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel (V1) [..G.Q], ULONG

 FUNCTION
   Luma bit depth of the coded image: 8, 10 or 12. It is taken from the pixi
   property of the image where the file has one and from its av1C box
   otherwise, and it is 0 until port 0 has been set up.

 NOTES
   This is the depth the image is coded with, not a pixel size of an output
   format. Port 1 carries the coded stream, which has no pixels at all; an
   application which wants the depth of the pixels it ends up with has to ask
   the decoder on the other side of the pipeline.

   An alpha item is coded separately and may have its own depth, which this
   class does not report separately. The decoder reads it from the stream.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Width, avif.decoder/MMA_Video_BitsPerPixel

avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_FrameCount

 NAME
   MMA_Video_FrameCount (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Returns 1, and 1 also before port 0 has been set up. A file gives one
   image, and an animated one gives the image its primary item reference
   names, which is why the count is never anything else.

 NOTES
   On MorphOS the multimedia framework stores this attribute in a LONG, and
   this class writes it as one. A QUAD variable on the receiving side is fine,
   but do not let the framework write a UQUAD into it: use the 32 bit form of
   the attribute macro.

avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Height

 NAME
   MMA_Video_Height (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Height of the image in pixels, from the ispe property of the primary item,
   and 0 until port 0 has been set up. The property is not optional: a file
   whose primary item has none is refused with MMERR_WRONG_DATA, so a
   successful setup always leaves a usable value here.

   This is the height of the coded image, which is the height of the decoded
   picture as well.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Width, avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Height

avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_UseAlpha

 NAME
   MMA_Video_UseAlpha (V1) [..G.Q], BOOL

 FUNCTION
   TRUE when the image has an alpha channel which is coded as an item of its
   own, FALSE otherwise and before port 0 has been set up. Such an item is
   recognised by the auxiliary type urn of its auxC property, or, when no
   item carries that urn, by being the item an 'auxl' reference of the primary
   item points at.

 NOTES
   An image whose alpha was premultiplied into the colour planes has no
   separate alpha item and reports FALSE here, even though it is an image
   with alpha. The premultiplied flag itself is in ai_AlphaPremul of
   MMA_ExtraData.

   The alpha stream is not emitted on port 1. It travels to avif.decoder
   through ai_AlphaES in MMA_ExtraData, which is the only path between the
   two classes, so an application which decodes AVIF itself has to take it
   from there.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_ExtraData, avif.decoder/MMA_Video_UseAlpha

avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Width

 NAME
   MMA_Video_Width (V1) [..G.Q], LONG

 FUNCTION
   Width of the image in pixels, from the ispe property of the primary item,
   and 0 until port 0 has been set up. The property is not optional: a file
   whose primary item has none is refused with MMERR_WRONG_DATA, so a
   successful setup always leaves a usable value here.

   This is the width of the coded image, which is the width of the decoded
   picture as well.

 SEE ALSO
   avif.demuxer/MMA_Video_Height, avif.decoder/MMA_Video_Width

avif.demuxer/MMM_Pull

 NAME
   MMM_Pull -- Pulls the AV1 elementary stream out of an AVIF file. (V1)

 SYNOPSIS
   ULONG DoMethod(Object *obj, MMM_Pull, ULONG port, APTR buffer, ULONG
   length);

 FUNCTION
   Port 1 hands out the AV1 OBU stream of the primary image item: the bytes of
   its extents in the order the location box lists them. They were copied out
   of the file during MMM_Setup on port 0, so the pull neither reads nor seeks
   the source. A pull returns as much of the rest of the stream as fits into
   length and a short count when the end of it is in the way; once the last
   byte has been handed out a pull returns 0 and reports MMERR_END_OF_DATA
   through MMA_ErrorCode. A pull on port 1 before port 0 has been set up
   answers the same way, because there is nothing to hand out yet.

   Port 0 is a passthrough to the source stream and returns the raw file.
   That is what the recognition code of this class uses to find the ftyp box,
   and the class restores what it peeked at. The passthrough is there for
   applications which want the file itself, not for this class: it reads the
   file through port 0 during its setup.

   A NULL buffer, a length of 0 or a port other than 0 or 1 is refused with
   MMERR_WRONG_ARGUMENTS.

 INPUTS
   obj - object to perform method on.
   port - number of port, 0 for the raw file or 1 for the AV1 stream.
   buffer - destination buffer for the data.
   length - amount of data to be pulled in *bytes*.

 RESULT
   Number of bytes pulled, secondary error information via MMA_ErrorCode.

 SEE ALSO
   multimedia.class/MMA_ErrorCode, avif.decoder/MMM_Pull


